For voltage-series feedback, \[R_{if}=R_i(1+A\beta)\qquad R_{of}=\frac{R_o}{1+A\beta}\] Given, \(A=100,\qquad \beta=0.99 \quad R_i = 1\text{K}\Omega \quad R_0 = 100\Omega\). Thus, \[1+A\beta=1+100(0.99)=100\] Input impedance becomes \[\begin{aligned}
R_{if}
=1\,\text{k}\Omega \times 100 
=100\,\text{k}\Omega
\end{aligned}\] Output impedance becomes \[\begin{aligned}
R_{of}
=\frac{100\,\Omega}{100} 
=1\,\Omega
\end{aligned}\] \[\boxed{\text{(C)}\quad 100\,\text{k}\Omega\ \text{and}\ 1\,\Omega}\]

For a single-pole system, the unit step response is of the form, \[v_o(t)=1-e^{-t/\tau}\] Rise time is defined from \(10\%\) to \(90\%\) response. \[t_r = -\tau\ln(0.1)-(-\tau\ln(0.9)\] \[t_r\approx 2.2\tau\] Also, upper cutoff frequency is \[f_H=\frac{1}{2\pi\tau}
=\frac{1}{2\pi(t_r/2.2)} 
=\frac{2.2}{2\pi t_r} 
\approx \frac{0.35}{t_r}\] \(\therefore\) For a single-pole system, the upper cutoff frequency is given by, \[f_H=\frac{0.35}{t_r}\] Given, \(t_r=35\,\text{ns}=35\times 10^{-9}\,\text{s}\) \[\therefore
f_H
=\frac{0.35}{35\times 10^{-9}} 
=10\times 10^6\,\text{Hz} 
=10\,\text{MHz}\] \[\boxed{\text{(B)}\quad 10}\]

At midband, \(C_1\) and \(C_2\) act as short circuits and the ideal current source offers infinite small-signal resistance The small-signal equivalent circuit is shown below <img src="img/311a828191b3f1b9.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Since \(\beta\) is very large, hence \(\alpha\approx1\) The intrinsic emitter resistance is \[r_e=\frac{V_T}{I_e}
=\frac{26\,\text{mV}}{0.5\,\text{mA}}
=52\,\Omega\] Hence the input resistance is, \(R_i=r_e=52\,\Omega\) The effective collector load is, \[R_L'=R_B ~\parallel ~R_L\;\Rightarrow  R_L'
=100\,\text{k}\Omega\parallel1\,\text{k}\Omega
=990\,\Omega\] The voltage gain of a common-base amplifier is, \[A_v=\frac{V_o}{V_i}=\frac{\alpha I_e R_L'}{r_e I_e}=\alpha\frac{R_L'}{r_e}
=\frac{990}{52}
\approx19.0\] To find the output resistance, set the input source to zero. Since \(r_o\rightarrow\infty\), looking into the collector only \(R_B\) is seen. Hence, \[R_o=R_B=100\,\text{k}\Omega\] \[\boxed{R_i\approx52\,\Omega}\] \[\boxed{A_v\approx19.0}\] \[\boxed{R_o=100\,\text{k}\Omega}\]

The transconductance of the transistor is \[g_m=\frac{I_C}{V_T}
=\frac{1\,\text{mA}}{26\,\text{mV}}
=\frac{1}{26}\,\text{A/V}\] The unity-gain cutoff frequency is \[f_T=\frac{g_m}{2\pi(C_{\pi}+C_{\mu})}\] Therefore \[C_{\pi}+C_{\mu}
=\frac{g_m}{2\pi f_T}
=\frac{1/26}{2\pi(400\times10^6)}
=15.3\,\text{pF}\] Hence \[C_{\pi}
=15.3-0.3
=15\,\text{pF}\] \[\boxed{(A)\;15\,\text{pF}}\]

For a single-pole amplifier, the relationship between rise time and bandwidth is \[BW=\frac{0.35}{t_r}\] Given, \(t_r=35\,\text{ns}=35\times10^{-9}\,\text{s}\) Therefore \[BW=\frac{0.35}{35\times10^{-9}}
=10\times10^6\,\text{Hz}
=10\,\text{MHz}\] \[\boxed{(B)\;10\,\text{MHz}}\]

